WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.857
Organizations generate enormous amounts of security-relevant data.

00:00:02.857 --> 00:00:06.402
Endpoint tools, identity providers, cloud platforms, applications, network devices,

00:00:06.402 --> 00:00:11.522
email systems, and SaaS services all produce events that may matter to defenders.

00:00:11.522 --> 00:00:15.066
Raw telemetry is not the same as defensive visibility.

00:00:15.066 --> 00:00:19.398
Without effective detections, security teams may struggle to identify suspicious activity,

00:00:19.398 --> 00:00:23.337
prioritize investigations, or understand what is happening across business systems.

00:00:23.337 --> 00:00:26.094
Detection engineering exists to bridge that gap.

00:00:26.094 --> 00:00:30.426
It transforms security telemetry into useful signals by combining data sources,

00:00:30.426 --> 00:00:33.577
behavioral hypotheses, context, investigation workflows, and operational feedback.

00:00:33.577 --> 00:00:36.334
The discipline also helps organizations manage attention.

00:00:36.334 --> 00:00:39.485
Analysts cannot investigate every event with equal depth.

00:00:39.485 --> 00:00:45.392
Good detection work helps reduce low-value noise while elevating activity that deserves a closer look.

00:00:45.392 --> 00:00:49.725
Detection engineering does not remove the need for analysts, incident responders,

00:00:49.725 --> 00:00:51.694
threat hunters, or security engineers.

00:00:51.694 --> 00:00:55.632
It supports them by making visibility more intentional, maintainable, measurable,

00:00:55.632 --> 00:00:58.389
and aligned with the organization's actual environment.

00:00:58.389 --> 00:01:01.540
It also helps teams decide where to invest.

00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:06.266
If a detection cannot be built because the required data is missing,

00:01:06.266 --> 00:01:07.842
that gap becomes visible.

00:01:07.842 --> 00:01:11.386
The team can then discuss logging, collection, enrichment, retention,

00:01:11.386 --> 00:01:15.325
or platform changes as part of a larger visibility roadmap.

00:01:15.325 --> 00:01:16.506
In that sense,

00:01:16.506 --> 00:01:21.232
detection engineering is both a security operations practice and a planning function.

00:01:21.232 --> 00:01:25.171
It helps leaders understand what the team can see today,

00:01:25.171 --> 00:01:29.897
what remains difficult to observe, and where better telemetry would improve response.

00:01:29.897 --> 00:01:34.229
This is why detection engineering is often tied to security maturity.

00:01:34.229 --> 00:01:40.137
It helps organizations move from reactive alert handling toward a managed visibility program with priorities,

00:01:40.137 --> 00:01:42.500
ownership, and a path for improvement.
